  "meta": {
    "title": "Rising-edge detector (Mealy)",
    "blurb": "Outputs 1 on exactly the clock ticks where the input goes from 0 to 1. A Mealy machine can react on the edge itself, so two states do it — the output depends on the state and the input together.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "0110",
        "out": "0100"
      },
      {
        "w": "1010",
        "out": "1010"
      },
      {
        "w": "0111001",
        "out": "0100001"
      },
      {
        "w": "1111",
        "out": "1000"
      },
      {
        "w": "0000",
        "out": "0000"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "hardware",
        "edge-detector",
        "moore-vs-mealy",
        "sequential-circuit"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "A staple of digital design courses, and the cleanest way to see the difference between the two kinds of state machine. Here the output sits on the **edges**: \"was low, reads 1\" is the only transition that outputs 1.\n\nThe Moore version — **Rising-edge detector (Moore)** — has to put the output on a *state*, so it needs a third state that means \"an edge just happened\", and its output appears one tick later. Same signal in, same pulses out, one state more.\n\nThe input is assumed to start low."
    }
  }
}
